+
+## 3. The permission and how to grant it
+
+| Key | Shown as | Effect |
+|---|---|---|
+| `PROJECTS:VIEW:ONLY_ASSIGNED` | Projects: View - Only Assigned | **Takes access away.** The user can only see and change projects they manage or are crew on. Needs `PROJECTS:VIEW`. |
+
+To set up a client account (e.g. an outside client):
+
+1. **Business settings > Permissions**: add a role (e.g. "Client") and tick:
+   - `Projects: View`
+   - `Projects: View - Only Assigned`
+   - `Projects - Project Assets: Create - Assign and unassign` and `... - Assign all business assets`
+   - `Projects - Project Crew: View`
+   - `Assets - Asset Types: View` (and any other asset view permissions they need)
+   Don't give it client, ledger, user-list or business-settings permissions unless they need them: those are
+   business-wide, as in stock AdamRMS.
+2. **Business settings > Users**: add the user with that role.
+3. On the client's project, either make them **crew** (Crew tab) or the **project manager**.
+
+Every other role is unchanged. Don't tick the new permission on the Administrator role.
+
+## 4. Upgrading to a new upstream release
+
+```bash
+git fetch upstream --tags
+# keep main identical to upstream
+git checkout main && git merge --ff-only upstream/main && git push origin main
+# bring the release into nopayne on a branch, so the e2e tests run on the pull request
+git checkout nopayne && git pull
+git checkout -b upgrade/vX.Y.Z
+git merge vX.Y.Z          # the upstream release tag
+```
+
+**Where conflicts are likely, and how to resolve them.** Each hook sits next to upstream code, so a conflict
+means upstream changed a line beside it. Take upstream's version of the surrounding code, then put the one hook
+line back in the same place:
+
+- `src/common/libs/Auth/instanceActions.php`: if upstream adds or edits permissions near `PROJECTS:VIEW`. Keep
+  both; our entry must stay inside `$instanceActions`.
+- `src/common/headSecure.php` and `src/api/apiHeadSecure.php`: our guard is the **last line**. If upstream adds
+  code at the end, the guard goes after it.
+- `src/common/head.php`: the `require_once` must stay after `$GLOBALS['AUTH'] = new bID;`.
+- Project queries (`list.php` ×2, `data.php`, `assets.php`, `calendar.php`, `ledger.php`, `user.php`,
+  `clients.php`, `vacancies.php`, `SearchProjects.php`): `nopayneProjectScopeWhere()` must be called after the
+  query's other `where()`s start and before its `get()`/`paginate()`.
+- `src/api/instances/new.php`, `e2e/setup/tenants.php`: if upstream changes how the Administrator / full-access
+  role is created, keep the `array_diff(..., ["PROJECTS:VIEW:ONLY_ASSIGNED"])`.
+- `.github/workflows/e2e-tests.yml`: keep `- nopayne` under `pull_request.branches`.
+
+Then check nothing was lost, and audit what upstream added:
+
+```bash
+git grep -n "No Payne fork" -- src e2e .github   # 24 hooks in src/ (plus the helper), 1 in e2e/setup/tenants.php, 1 in e2e-tests.yml
+# New or changed files that take a project ID or list projects need a look (see the audit table in section 2)
+git diff --name-only vOLD vX.Y.Z -- src | xargs grep -lE "projects_id|join\(\"projects\"|assetsAssignments|crewAssignments|payments_id|projectsVacantRoles|s3files_id" 2>/dev/null
+```
+
+If upstream adds an endpoint whose project-ish parameter isn't in `NOPAYNE_PROJECT_SCOPE_PARAMS` (for example a
+new name, or `id` meaning a project), add it to `NOPAYNE_PROJECT_SCOPE_SCRIPT_PARAMS` or
+`NOPAYNE_PROJECT_SCOPE_PARAMS`. If it lists projects, add a `nopayneProjectScopeWhere();` hook.
+
+**Re-run the e2e tests.** Push the branch and open a pull request into `nopayne`; `.github/workflows/e2e-tests.yml`
+runs the whole suite, including `e2e/authenticated/nopayne-project-scope.spec.ts`. To run them locally (PHP 8.3,
+MySQL 8, see `AGENTS.md`):
+
+```bash
+composer install
+cd e2e && npm ci && npx playwright install chromium
+npx playwright test                                            # everything
+npx playwright test authenticated/nopayne-project-scope.spec.ts  # just ours
+```
+
+Merge when green, then tag and deploy (section 6).
+
+**Checklist after an upgrade** (on staging, or production right after deploying). Log in as the client user
+(restricted role, crew on one project) and check:
+
+- [ ] Sidebar, dashboard calendar, **Projects** page, business calendar and search show only their project(s).
+- [ ] Their project opens and shows its gear with prices.
+- [ ] **Assets** page: the "Project to assign assets to" dropdown lists only their project(s); adding an asset
+      with the basket button works, and removing it works.
+- [ ] Another project's URL, `/project/?id=<other id>`, gives a 404.
+- [ ] The business calendar page shows no iCal subscription URL with a key in it.
+
+And from the browser's developer console while logged in as the client (each must return
+`{"result":false,"error":{"code":"AUTH-ERROR",...}}`; `OTHER` is a project they aren't on, `ASSET` a free asset):
+
+```js
+const post = (u, d) => fetch("/api/" + u, {method: "POST", body: new URLSearchParams(d)}).then(r => r.json());
+await post("projects/data.php", {id: OTHER});
+await post("projects/assets/assign.php", {projects_id: OTHER, assets_id: ASSET});
+await post("projects/changeName.php", {projects_id: OTHER, projects_name: "x"});
+await post("projects/list.php", {});   // result true, but only their project(s)
+```
+
+Finally log in as an admin and check you still see every project.
+
+## 5. `patches/`
+
+`patches/` holds `git format-patch v1.205.6..<last feature commit> -o patches/`: every commit of ours except the
+commit that adds `patches/` itself. To re-apply the fork to a fresh checkout of an upstream release:
+
+```bash
+git clone https://github.com/adam-rms/adam-rms.git && cd adam-rms
+git checkout -b nopayne vX.Y.Z
+git am /path/to/patches/*.patch     # on conflicts: fix, git add, git am --continue (see section 4)
+```
+
+Regenerate it whenever our commits change. After an upgrade merge, `git format-patch` from the new tag would
+include the merge, so export the feature commits themselves instead, e.g. on a scratch branch:
+`git checkout -b scratch vX.Y.Z && git cherry-pick <our commits, oldest first>` (fixing conflicts as in section 4),
+then `rm -r patches && git format-patch vX.Y.Z..scratch -o patches/` and commit `patches/` on `nopayne`.
+
+## 6. Building and deploying
+
+**Image:** `ghcr.io/jopdop30/adam-rms`, built from the repo `Dockerfile` (unchanged from upstream: PHP 8.3 +
+Apache, runs `migrate.sh` on start) by `.github/workflows/nopayne-image.yml`, for `linux/amd64`.
+
+**To build a release:** tag the `nopayne` commit you want, as `nopayne-v<upstream version>-<n>`:
+
+```bash
+git checkout nopayne && git pull
+git tag nopayne-v1.205.6-1
+git push origin nopayne-v1.205.6-1
+```
+
+The workflow pushes `ghcr.io/jopdop30/adam-rms:nopayne-v1.205.6-1` and moves `:nopayne-latest` to it. The tag
+name is also written to `src/version/TAG.txt` in the image.
+
+GitHub makes a new package private by default. Either make it public (GitHub > your profile > Packages >
+adam-rms > Package settings > Change visibility), or give Dokploy a registry login for `ghcr.io` with a token
+that has `read:packages`.
+
+**Dokploy:** in the AdamRMS compose file, replace
+
+```yaml
+    image: ghcr.io/adam-rms/adam-rms:latest
+```
+
+with the explicit tag:
+
+```yaml
+    image: ghcr.io/jopdop30/adam-rms:nopayne-v1.205.6-1
+```
+
+and redeploy. **Always deploy an explicit tag**, never `:latest` or `:nopayne-latest`, so you know what's
+running and can roll back by changing the tag. Nothing else in the compose (environment, database, volumes)
+changes. There are no migrations in the fork, so the database works with either image. **But** stock AdamRMS
+ignores `PROJECTS:VIEW:ONLY_ASSIGNED`: if you ever roll back to `ghcr.io/adam-rms/adam-rms`, restricted users
+immediately see every project. Suspend client accounts (or take `Projects: View` off their role) before doing that.
